# A program that watches and switches: a sensor gives the program a number it cannot set, an if with
# a threshold decides a switch, and the switch changes what the sensor reads next. At the research
# hut under the midnight sun the heater keeps the hut warm and a lamp comes on in fog.
lesson coding-sensors-and-feedback v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=3 subject=coding levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A program that watches and switches"
  goal "Work out what a program that reads a sensor and switches a heater does to the reading, minute by minute."

  look {
    scene 36x27 {
      program p code=["repeat 20", "  if temp is less than 18", "    switch heater on", "  if temp is more than 21", "    switch heater off", "  wait 1"] text=true sense=["temp starts 15 changes -1 heater 2"] at=canvas(0, 0)
      thermometer th from=0 to=30 step=10 value=15 unit="°C" right-of=p gap=1
      switched sw names=["heater"] on=[] below=th gap=1
      midnightsun ms suns=5 midnight=1 below=p gap=1
    }
    say """
      The research hut on the ice shelf loses 1 degree a minute. Its program reads temp from the
      thermometer, a sensor: the program can ask about it but cannot set it. Below 18 it switches the
      heater on, and above 21 it switches it off. With the heater on the hut gains 2 degrees a
      minute and still loses 1, so it warms by 1. Each wait 1 is a minute. What the program switches
      changes what it reads next, and that loop is called feedback.
      """
  }
  do {
    show coding.sense-read d=4 v=6 easy-v=7 hard-v=4
    level easy {
      worked coding.sense-minute s=12 h=2 m=5 level=medium
    }
    show coding.sense-minute s=13 h=2 m=5 easy-s=12 easy-m=3 hard-h=3 hard-m=8
    show coding.sense-not t=18 v=18 easy-v=16 hard-t=17
    show coding.sense-count k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show coding.sense-flow k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show coding.sense-lamp k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show coding.sense-high k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show coding.sense-minute s=14 h=3 m=3 easy-s=14 easy-h=2 easy-m=5 hard-s=12 hard-m=8
    level hard {
      show coding.sense-not t=18 v=19 level=medium
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show coding.sense-fewer k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show coding.sense-most k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "A sensor gives the program a number, an if compares it with a threshold and switches something, and what is switched changes the next reading."
  }

  grown-ups """
    A thermostat is the plainest example of control with feedback: a sensor reads the room, the
    program compares the reading with a threshold, switches the heater, and the heater changes the
    reading the program sees next. The program can ask about a sensor but cannot set it. The common
    slips are forgetting that the hut still cools while the heater is on, expecting the heater to
    switch off exactly at the top number when it waits until the reading is more than it, and reading
    not more than 18 as less than 18. In the child's working, look for a table with a row a minute and
    columns for the reading and for the heater. If the child is stuck, play it with a real
    thermometer in a glass of water and a cup of warm water to pour in when it drops below a line;
    stay another day if the table still skips the heater column. Off the page, find the thermostats at
    home (the fridge, the oven, the hot water) and ask what each reads and what it switches. The
    two-star question compares three changes to the thresholds without running each one, and the
    three-star question finds the highest switch-on number that keeps the heater to a set number of
    starts. On screen, Run shows the thermometer's reading and the heater change minute by minute, once
    a question has been answered; on paper every question is worked from the program and the numbers
    printed with it. This meets China's 2022 standard
    for grades five and six, 过程与控制 内容要求 3 and 4, 「反馈是过程与控制中的重要手段」 (feedback is an
    important means of control) and 「连续量可以经由阈值判断形成开关量」 (a continuous quantity becomes an
    on or off one through a threshold), and Japan's 2017 course of study, 理科 第3の2(2), where a
    program is considered as acting on the conditions it is given.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the readings asked about are well away from the thresholds, the hut warms by 1 degree a minute with the heater on and one minute-by-minute table is worked in the teacher's pen, the minutes asked are 3 or 5, the heater comes on twice, the empty shape switches the heater on, and the fog is the plainest. The two-star question starts from 18 and 21, and the three-star allows the heater three starts."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the readings sit on the thresholds, where less than and not more than differ, the hut warms by 2 degrees a minute and the table runs to minute 8, past the top and back down, the heater warms by 2 in the counting and warmest questions, the empty shape is the wait that lets a minute pass, and a second not question is added. The two-star question starts from 17 and 22 with a stronger heater, and the three-star uses the stronger heater with three starts."
  }
}
